# Copy to .env before running `docker compose up`.
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# One value here is a secret - API_ACCESS_TOKEN, and you have to set it. The
# service stores nothing, so there is no datastore to protect and no key to
# hold; everything else below tunes inference, bounds a container, or says
# which build to serve. Never commit a filled-in .env, and never write a real
# value into this file.

# Where the NER weights are on this machine. They are mounted into the backend
# read-only at /app/models rather than copied into the image, so a retrained
# model is picked up by `docker compose restart backend` with no rebuild. The
# directory holds the files `from_pretrained` reads - config.json,
# model.safetensors, tokenizer.json, tokenizer_config.json.
#
# Write it as an absolute path, or as a relative one starting with `./`. Compose
# reads a bare `models` as the name of a Docker volume rather than as a
# directory, and it does not expand `~` - `~/models` creates a directory
# literally named `~` beside this file. A relative path resolves against the
# directory holding docker-compose.yml, not against your shell's.
#
# Every one of those mistakes fails the same quiet way: a path that does not
# exist is created empty by Docker rather than refused, so the backend starts,
# fails the load, and answers 503 on /readyz and on the analysis routes. The
# interface reports that as the service being unavailable and says no more,
# because it says nothing about configuration to a clinician. If that warning is
# on screen, check this path first, then `docker compose logs backend`.
#
# Point it only at a directory holding the weights, owned by whoever runs the
# deployment: whatever it names is mounted into the backend's filesystem.
MODEL_DIR=./backend/models

# Threads torch may use for one inference. Left unset, docker-compose.yml
# defaults it to BACKEND_CPU_LIMIT below, because torch reads the host's core
# count rather than the container's CPU quota and would otherwise start a
# thread per host core inside a two-core allowance. Set it to 0 to get torch's
# own default back, or lower to leave the host responsive.
NER_INFERENCE_THREADS=2

# How many documents may be inside the model at once. One by default: a second
# set of activations is what puts a small machine into swap. Above one, the same
# model object is called from several threads, which transformers does not
# document as safe - prefer running more backend processes. If you do raise it,
# pin NER_INFERENCE_THREADS at the same time, or each inference claims every
# core and they contend.
NER_MAX_CONCURRENT_INFERENCES=1

# Set to `development` only to expose the mock endpoints. Never in a deployment.
APP_ENV=production

# Which browser origins may call the API, comma-separated, each written as
# scheme://host[:port] with no trailing slash and no port the scheme already
# implies - both are dropped rather than refused, since a browser omits them.
# This is the other half of NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL below: that one tells the
# browser where the API is, this one tells the API which page is allowed to ask.
# This list is enforced on the server, not only advertised to browsers. A
# request to the analysis routes carrying an `Origin` that is not on it is
# refused with 403 before the body is read, so another site cannot drive an
# upload through a visitor's browser and spend this deployment's compute on
# documents of its choosing. CORS alone did not stop that: it withholds the
# answer from the attacking page, but the request was still analysed.
#
# It is still not authentication. A client that is not a browser writes whatever
# it likes in an `Origin` header, and a request that carries none is let through
# - the proxy in front sends none, and neither does the healthcheck. What
# authenticates a caller is API_ACCESS_TOKEN below, which is required.
#
# Serve the interface from
# anywhere other than the default and both have to move, or the browser drops
# every answer. Left unset it stays on the local frontend origin rather than
# widening - the API answers with credentials, and a browser refuses a
# credentialed response allowed to `*` anyway, so the wildcard is rejected at
# startup instead of quietly breaking every call.
CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# Which host address the backend's port 8000 is published on. The default is
# loopback, so the API is reachable from the machine running the stack and from
# anything on the Docker network - a reverse proxy included - and from nowhere
# else. That is deliberate: `POST /api/analyze` ingests clinical documents, and
# while API_ACCESS_TOKEN below is now required, a shared credential is one secret
# that identifies nobody - publishing the port on a public interface exposes it
# to everyone who can scan for it, and to unlimited guessing.
#
# Set it to 0.0.0.0 only if you have decided the API should answer every address
# this host has, and read the two lines above again before you do. It does not
# affect the frontend, which is published on all interfaces as before.
BACKEND_BIND_ADDRESS=127.0.0.1

# A shared credential required on POST /api/analyze and POST /api/analyze/stream.
# REQUIRED. There is no default and no unconfigured mode: `docker compose up`
# refuses to start without it, and so does the backend.
#
# It used to be optional, and that was the whole security posture of every
# deployment that did not read this file: an unconfigured service answered
# anyone who could reach the port while looking, from the outside, exactly like
# a configured one. Now the analysis routes refuse every request that does not
# carry `Authorization: Bearer <this value>`, with a fixed 401 that says nothing
# else. /healthz and /readyz are never affected - the container healthcheck and
# the interface's availability poll both need them.
#
# What it is: one secret shared by every caller, which tells the API that a
# caller is one the operator set up. What it is not: a login. It identifies
# nobody, cannot be revoked for one client, and does not survive being given to
# a second person. Authenticating a *human* belongs in a proxy in front of this
# service - which can then add this header on the way through.
#
# THE BROWSER INTERFACE IN THIS STACK CANNOT USE IT. The page calls the API
# directly, so any credential it could send would be in the bundle, in the
# network tab, and readable by every visitor. Setting this without a proxy that
# injects the header breaks the frontend. deploy/README.md has the shape that
# works.
#
# Generate it, do not choose it - the API refuses a value under 32 characters at
# startup, because the caller it keeps out is the one who can retry forever:
#   python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"
#
# A blank value is refused exactly as a missing one. There is no way to spell
# "no credential" here that starts a server.
#
# Keep it out of git, out of shell history, and out of any log. A .env holding
# it is a secret file: `chmod 600 .env`.
API_ACCESS_TOKEN=

# What each container may take from the host. The backend holds the model
# resident, so its limit is the one that matters. Measured: 470 MiB idle,
# 504 MiB peak across a batch of five long documents; it starts and analyses at
# 512m with nothing to spare. The default is roughly four times the peak,
# because a large PDF is expanded in memory by its parser. The RAM-backed /tmp
# that uploads spool into is charged against this limit too.
BACKEND_MEMORY_LIMIT=2g

# Inference is CPU-bound and this is the setting that decides how long a batch
# takes. Keep NER_INFERENCE_THREADS in step with it: measured on a 14-core host
# under a 2-core quota, five long documents took 11s with the two matched and
# 215s with torch left to pick its own thread count.
BACKEND_CPU_LIMIT=2

FRONTEND_MEMORY_LIMIT=512m
FRONTEND_CPU_LIMIT=1

# The URL the browser uses to reach the backend. Baked into the frontend bundle
# at build time, so changing it requires a rebuild of the frontend image.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000
